From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 09:47:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] adbd: authorize the host key from ro.boot.kernarium.adb_key

Kernarium's host application is the adb host of the guest it runs. It
keeps one key per guest in its own keychain and cannot answer the "Allow
USB debugging" prompt of a guest it drives, nor write the framework's
/data/misc/adb/adb_keys, which the framework expires and empties when
the user revokes USB debugging authorizations.

The host passes the base64 adb public key on the kernel command line at
every boot, and init exposes it as the immutable property
ro.boot.kernarium.adb_key. IteratePublicKeys, the one wrapper over
libadbd_auth's key files, now offers that value to its callback first
when it is not empty, reading it once, and stops when the callback does.
The AUTH signature check, the TLS client CA list and the TLS certificate
check all iterate through it, so every path sees the same keys.
libadbd_auth is unchanged and its key files are consulted as before.

Trust model: the host owns the guest's whole disk, so the key gives it
nothing it lacks. The guest's user cannot revoke the key, which is
deliberate. The value is a public key, so a guest application that
reads the property learns nothing it can use.
---
 daemon/auth.cpp | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/daemon/auth.cpp b/daemon/auth.cpp
index 8e6b27d884813f3e6e11c3b0e92c2243d47c6a81..239560670d100a48862ef920cb502a433b006adb 100644
--- a/daemon/auth.cpp
+++ b/daemon/auth.cpp
@@ -34,6 +34,7 @@
 #include <adbd_auth.h>
 #include <android-base/file.h>
 #include <android-base/no_destructor.h>
+#include <android-base/properties.h>
 #include <android-base/strings.h>
 #include <crypto_utils/android_pubkey.h>
 #include <openssl/obj_mac.h>
@@ -123,6 +124,19 @@ static atransport* transport_from_callback_arg(void* id) {
 }
 
 static void IteratePublicKeys(std::function<bool(std::string_view public_key)> f) {
+    // The host application's own key, read once. Kernarium's host application is the adb host of
+    // the guest it runs: it keeps a key of its own per guest and passes the base64 public key as
+    // the immutable ro.boot.kernarium.adb_key of every boot, so the guest needs neither the
+    // "Allow USB debugging" prompt nor a write to /data/misc/adb/adb_keys, which the framework
+    // owns, expires and empties. The host owns the guest's disk, so the key grants it nothing it
+    // lacks, and the guest's user cannot revoke it. The property holds a public key only. It
+    // comes first, so that every caller (the AUTH signature check, the TLS client CA list and the
+    // TLS certificate check) sees the same keys.
+    static const std::string host_key =
+            android::base::GetProperty("ro.boot.kernarium.adb_key", "");
+    if (!host_key.empty() && !f(host_key)) {
+        return;
+    }
     adbd_auth_get_public_keys(
             auth_ctx,
             [](void* opaque, const char* public_key, size_t len) {
